column1:
Composition means a class HOLDS a reference to another object as a field (a "has-a" relationship). Inheritance means a class EXTENDS another class (an "is-a" relationship). Looking at real code from this course:

public class FeeDecorator extends PaymentDecorator {
    ...
}

public class PaymentDecorator implements IPayment {
    protected IPayment ip;
    ...
}

Which relationship does PaymentDecorator's ip field represent — composition or inheritance?,"Composition — PaymentDecorator holds an IPayment object as a field, it doesn't extend it","Inheritance — PaymentDecorator ""is-a"" IPayment object",Neither — fields are unrelated to either concept,Both — Java doesn't distinguish between the two,,1,A field holding a reference to another object is the definition of composition — no extends keyword is involved here.,1

Same file, different relationship:

public class FeeDecorator extends PaymentDecorator {

Is this composition or inheritance, and why?,"Neither — extends only applies to interfaces, not classes","Inheritance — FeeDecorator uses the extends keyword, meaning it ""is-a"" PaymentDecorator, gaining its fields and methods directly rather than holding a separate PaymentDecorator object",Both — extends always implies an internal field reference too,Composition — FeeDecorator is just reusing PaymentDecorator's code,,2,"extends is the defining keyword for inheritance in Java — it establishes an is-a relationship, not a has-a one.",1

Real code from class-21:

public class EmailSubscriber extends User {
    private String email;
    ...
}

Is EmailSubscriber's relationship to User composition or inheritance?,"Composition — EmailSubscriber has an email field, which counts as composition",Composition — because EmailSubscriber overrides notifyUser(),"Inheritance — EmailSubscriber extends User directly, gaining User's fields and methods as its own, rather than storing a User object inside itself",It's ambiguous without seeing User's own code,,3,"The extends keyword on User is what determines the relationship type here, not the presence of EmailSubscriber's own unrelated email field.",1

Real code from class-19/20:

public class MediaPlayer {
    private IState state = StopState.getInstance();
    ...
}

Is MediaPlayer's relationship to IState composition or inheritance?,"Inheritance — MediaPlayer ""is-a"" state",Both — since state is assigned a concrete value in the field declaration,Neither — this is an example of the Proxy relationship instead,Composition — MediaPlayer holds an IState reference as a field

Real code from class-22:

public abstract class Level {
    abstract IEnemy createEnemy();
    public void startLevel(){
        IEnemy enemy = createEnemy();
        enemy.attack();
    }
}
public class ForestLevel extends Level {
    IEnemy createEnemy() { return new Goblin(); }
}

This code uses BOTH inheritance and composition — identify each. ForestLevel's relationship to Level is one; startLevel()'s local enemy variable relationship to IEnemy is the other.,"Both relationships are composition, since neither uses the extends keyword directly on IEnemy","ForestLevel extends Level is inheritance (is-a)

Real code from class-25 (Composite, file system example):

public class Folder implements FileSystemItem {
    private List<FileSystemItem> children;
    ...
}

Folder implements FileSystemItem (like File does) AND holds a List<FileSystemItem> as a field. Which relationship is which, and why does Folder need BOTH simultaneously rather than just one?,The List field is inheritance since it's typed to the same interface Folder implements,Both are the same relationship expressed two different ways,"implements FileSystemItem establishes the is-a relationship (so a Folder can be treated exactly like a File wherever FileSystemItem is expected, including being added inside another Folder)

Real code from class-25 (Facade):

public class CheckoutFacade {
    private PaymentValidator validator;
    private FraudCheckService fraudCheck;
    private PaymentGateway gateway;
    private NotificationService notifier;
    ...
}

CheckoutFacade has FOUR fields, each a different concrete class, with no extends anywhere. Is this composition, and if so, why does CheckoutFacade use four separate fields rather than one shared interface field (the way Folder uses one List<FileSystemItem>)?,Composition only counts when there's exactly one field

Real code from class-25 (Proxy):

public class DocumentProxy implements Document {
    private String filename;
    private RealDocument realDocument;
    ...
}

Why does DocumentProxy hold a RealDocument field via composition instead of doing DocumentProxy extends RealDocument?,"RealDocument's constructor eagerly loads the file (loadFromDisk()) — if DocumentProxy extends RealDocument, constructing a DocumentProxy would ALSO trigger RealDocument's constructor (since a subclass constructor always invokes its parent's), defeating the entire purpose of delaying that expensive load

Real code from class-20:

public class PlayState implements IState {
    private static final PlayState singleton = new PlayState();
    private PlayState(){}
    public static PlayState getInstance(){ return singleton; }
    ...
    public void pause(MediaPlayer player) {
        player.setState(PauseState.getInstance());
    }
}

PlayState.pause() takes a MediaPlayer player parameter and calls player.setState(...). Is this a composition relationship between PlayState and MediaPlayer? Why or why not, given PlayState never stores player as a field?,"Yes — this is composition because MediaPlayer also holds an IState field, making the relationship mutual","This is inheritance, since PlayState implements IState and MediaPlayer also references IState","No — this is neither composition nor inheritance in the strict sense

Given the previous question's distinction — that composition specifically means HOLDING a reference as a field over time, not just receiving one as a parameter — look at Processor from the Adapter-pattern folder:

public class Processor {
    private IPayment ip;
    public Processor(IPayment ip) {
        this.ip = ip;
    }
    public void process() {
        ip.pay();
    }
}

Does Processor demonstrate true composition by this stricter definition?,"No — since ip is passed in through the constructor, this is actually a parameter, not composition","Yes, but only because process() has no parameters of its own","No — composition requires the held type to be a concrete class, not an interface","Yes — ip is stored as a field (private IPayment ip) via the constructor, persisting beyond any single method call, which is exactly what distinguishes true composition from merely passing a parameter into one method",,4,"The constructor parameter is just how the field gets its initial value — what makes it composition is that the value is then stored as a field, not that it arrived via a parameter.",1

A teammate refactors EmailSubscriber (currently extends User) into using composition instead — holding a User user field rather than extending it:

public class EmailSubscriber implements ISubscriber {
    private User user;
    private String email;
    ...
    public void notifyUser(String message) {
        System.out.println("Email sending started to " + email);
        user.notifyUser(message);
        System.out.println("Email sending completed");
    }
}

Functionally, does this produce the same behavior as the original extends User version?,Yes — user.notifyUser(message) achieves the same effect as super.notifyUser(message) did in the inheritance version

Real code from class-16/17 (Template Method):

public abstract class BeverageMaker {
    abstract void brew();
    abstract void addCondiments();
    void boilWater() { ... }
    void pourInACup() { ... }
    public void makeBeverage() {
        boilWater();
        brew();
        pourInACup();
        addCondiments();
    }
}

Template Method relies on inheritance (extends BeverageMaker) specifically, rather than composition. Given everything discussed about composition's flexibility (swappable at runtime, narrow exposed surface), why does Template Method deliberately choose inheritance here instead?,Inheritance was chosen purely because Java doesn't support composition for methods with no return value,Composition is impossible to use whenever abstract methods are involved,"Template Method's whole point is that makeBeverage()'s fixed sequence (boilWater, brew, pourInACup, addCondiments) is shared, unchangeable structure that EVERY beverage must follow — inheritance is the natural fit because subclasses aren't swapping in a different BeverageMaker at runtime, they're permanently specializing exactly two fixed steps (brew, addCondiments) while keeping the rest identical, which is precisely what ""is-a specialized version of"" (inheritance) captures, not ""has-a swappable behavior"" (composition)","Template Method could have used composition instead with zero behavioral difference, and the choice was arbitrary",,3,This is why Template Method and State choose differently: fixed permanent specialization fits inheritance
